#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "7a6fbfc8-cd6d-57fe-bcab-ab68339683bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-155" :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-12$ | $-7$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $15$ | $21$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x) - f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=16.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(16) & ≈\\frac{f(16)-f(16-3)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(16)-f(13)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{21-15}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "cdf8854d-14ea-5564-81cc-4e5098f1acc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-156" :question/problem "A race car's position along a straight track, measured in meters from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ | $1.8$ | $2.0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $1.3$ | $2.7$ | $2.9$ | $3.5$ | $5.0$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ when $t=1.6$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t) - s(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=1.6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(1.6) & ≈\\frac{s(1.6)-s(1.6-0.2)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(1.6)-s(1.4)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.9-2.7}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2}{0.2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at the time $t=1.6,$ the velocity is approximately $1\\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092084 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "6e4ab84e-f722-557e-9740-25b3a0b3d660" :question/math-academy-id "e-6104" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e3f77e5b7d491e3ee43c8f8150065282d2abc259e3a88751b93ff15b27f492a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x) - g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=6.$ Applying the above formula with $x=6$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{g(6)-g(6-4)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092085 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "fb3d12e8-e413-5300-8d3f-991ddfd2a893" :question/math-academy-id "e-6105" :question/problem "A factory produces fans. The total cost of producing $x$ fans, in dollars, is given by the function $C(x).$  The graph of $C(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ad/ad3fae3694c4df8269be9a54350aabf83368b3152ea69eb2b9d1ac76e702823f.png)\n\nApproximate the marginal cost $C'(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nC'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{C(x) - C(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3.$ Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{C(3)-C(3-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{C(3)-C(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-20}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the marginal cost is $\\[math]5$ per fan."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "942b21b2-98f9-5164-9176-34228edf9f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "942b21b2-98f9-5164-9176-34228edf9f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315943} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3a0ff7a-ff90-5202-ab60-d69932d64cb8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c44d4d0b-eb44-5f39-a106-5b66e352fb91" :question/math-academy-id "q-343788" :question/problem "A distance function $s(t)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.02$ | $0.04$ | $0.06$ | $0.08$ | $0.10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $9$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=0.06$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{s}^{′}(0.06)≈$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t)-s(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=0.06.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.02.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(0.06) & ≈\\frac{s(0.06)-s(0.06-0.02)}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(0.06)-s(0.04)}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-5}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0.02} \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8443d9dc-35f0-5287-a16f-99b3e3adb0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8443d9dc-35f0-5287-a16f-99b3e3adb0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315853} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4fb7757-9b7b-5def-a523-240ba832d6f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e6d430f-227b-5241-8f12-1f8ddddf88bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343869" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd917228308b839be77110b07e32fa0154350c290920628400bd1f20a34160b5.png)\n\nA factory tracks the number of defective items remaining after a recall. The number of defective items after $x$ days, in hundreds, is given by the function $N(x).$ The graph of $N(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=15$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=10$ and $x=15.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=15$ is\n{{field-1}} hundred items per day." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{N(x)-N(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=15.$  Applying the above formula with $x=15$ and $h=5$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{′}(15) & ≈\\frac{N(15)-N(15-5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{N(15)-N(10)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{72-76}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=15$ is $-\\frac{4}{5}$ hundred items per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca436a4-c1c3-5d20-829e-ecb4501a591b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327747} {:answer/id #uuid "7b69b5af-e937-574e-8c48-2ea9e42d26a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327993} {:answer/id #uuid "8e618b33-0dd4-5334-902a-cb58b9ce9b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328168} {:answer/id #uuid "abfd0dc8-8e58-5241-a1a3-18a9b73e55be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328426} {:answer/id #uuid "f331c5e5-09c6-56ea-a378-73a26d6e18b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{liter}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ca436a4-c1c3-5d20-829e-ecb4501a591b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327747} :answer-field/id #uuid "d81bec37-e38f-50f3-8bdd-1660add3bd65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1dcc4d5a-d1fd-535c-85f6-3226b651cb5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-47357" :question/problem "![](images/64/64e35c559b233a28c5913ffa37ad949653ad2b46a0e10db639626b8575b7a263.png)\n\nAn oil tank is leaking $q(t)$ liters of oil, where $t>0$ is the time in hours since the leak started.\n The graph of $q(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=2.$ Applying the above formula with $t=2$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2)-q(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the rate of leaking is $2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a131aa90-94dc-5185-a5e5-83284e3a99e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090491} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f8f630-8722-5c01-a733-be69f3e2e761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090492} {:answer/id #uuid "156cfa70-8e2e-586c-a30c-1c5145dc0ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090493} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbc9ee0-c0e1-5749-8323-7087eb7b9c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090494} {:answer/id #uuid "639fb1d7-c75e-5892-a472-69ec85ed13cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090495}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cf497cdc-27ef-542c-8091-5977b7b78534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090490}] :question/id #uuid "58be26c8-7063-5aa8-be3d-09b3c442a08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-47358" :question/problem "![](images/40/40faa5a8b9bc08fcef23477a938c24d9aa6ebbc987b8eaeb3175cdec47bc00b3.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g^{′}(x)$ at the point $x = 4$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28130ceb-9078-5459-b297-e8134c62e13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336407} {:answer/id #uuid "66d3ff52-3de8-5ba0-a194-c66545a47d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336950} {:answer/id #uuid "74ba232c-4a6a-5215-87c9-e60c1f3a979d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337083} {:answer/id #uuid "a8735acd-9466-5c36-9ed4-b6b844e738b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337553} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d2f2d0-af24-550f-bcb3-be05cd751a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8735acd-9466-5c36-9ed4-b6b844e738b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337553} :answer-field/id #uuid "849c1abd-c41d-541d-a395-847cc4abb634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53e91cd6-f323-51a3-9858-aa9ad095941d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72052" :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.9$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.9$ |\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=-1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ≈\\frac{f(-1)-f(-1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(-1)-f(-2)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1-0.4}{1} \\\\  & =-0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13767acd-649b-59be-ad27-203eee29afee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336216} {:answer/id #uuid "5197e430-90c9-5cc2-8140-0470c526be0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336779} {:answer/id #uuid "8867342d-49be-595d-a3b3-31f8af42aacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337263} {:answer/id #uuid "bb66e87e-c032-525c-9c3b-89ba61ac1e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337693} {:answer/id #uuid "f0067241-e516-5e4a-9ee6-3a96e0452dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5197e430-90c9-5cc2-8140-0470c526be0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336779} :answer-field/id #uuid "772d8df6-b248-5b1e-af6b-e5904a9e4471" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b201471a-0c40-5010-8df2-c681645f2579" :question/math-academy-id "q-72067" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a93150906c0681b8654370267fb918076338fa8ab5bf68f50a0062a4ab34af1d.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=-1.$ Applying the above formula with $x=-1$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ≈\\frac{f(-1)-f(-1-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(-1)-f(-3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-(-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018bb92a-ff14-5746-9c6c-de3751c4a874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336045} {:answer/id #uuid "704b6aa8-de8a-5999-97b5-d026d16e7344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337029} {:answer/id #uuid "74aded53-6144-5282-acc4-81491103b6e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337082} {:answer/id #uuid "85463ad5-4ca8-574e-8a00-f30ad6899626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337232} {:answer/id #uuid "deec633d-8558-5fb3-bdaa-b2c95434ecaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "018bb92a-ff14-5746-9c6c-de3751c4a874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336045} :answer-field/id #uuid "b192d529-15af-5900-b6f2-5222f3ecc14b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de00370a-95a3-557d-bc46-f1d4bc108705" :question/math-academy-id "q-72075" :question/problem "An oil tank is leaking. The volume of oil in the tank, in liters, after $t$ hours is given by the function $q(t).$ The values of $q(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $q(t)$ | $200$ | $192$ | $186$ | $182$ | $180$ |\n\nApproximate the rate at which the volume of oil in the tank is changing when $t=2$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2)-q(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =186-192 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the rate of change of the volume is $-6$ liters per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d9dee40-eb74-576e-92c5-76c16cb64c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352532} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa3e1a7-2a85-5828-93dd-859c684add2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353510} {:answer/id #uuid "e349ed78-175a-5ce2-a1ed-1e87a2ab1ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "800\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353546} {:answer/id #uuid "edb061a8-04c3-5e4b-b27e-be6779beea3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353639} {:answer/id #uuid "f918e849-9416-5f20-87b4-5595578edb5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edb061a8-04c3-5e4b-b27e-be6779beea3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353639} :answer-field/id #uuid "5976a8f6-c771-532d-a97d-05e911bfc791" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "099eb4d8-6750-5b5d-884c-e755a070207d" :question/math-academy-id "q-86484" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fcdf0d68d78cba445d1e3ecfeec237ee4e33d7c74cb3b292593fe8b717fe6b8.png)\n\nPeter notices that the total number of eggs produced on his farm $t$ weeks after the beginning of his observations is given by the function $N(t).$ The graph of $N(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the number of produced eggs increases when $t=5$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{N(t)-N(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=5.$ Applying the above formula with $t=5$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{′}(5) & ≈\\frac{N(5)-N(5-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{N(5)-N(4)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{800-600}{1} \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the rate of increase is $200\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}.$"}]}
